}
```

This implementation uses a two-pointer technique to iterate over the two arrays. The first pointer, j, starts at the beginning of the second array, while the second pointer, i, starts at the beginning of the first array.

The code then checks if the current element of the first array (a[i]) is less than the current element of the second array (b[j]). If it is, the second pointer, j, is incremented until b[j] is greater than a[i] or until there are no more elements to compare.

If b[j] is greater than or equal to a[i], the code checks if b[j] is equal to a[i]. If it is, the code displays "Common element is: " and breaks the current loop.

After the loop is complete, the program will exit the main method.

Note that the arrays should be sorted before applying this algorithm. This can be done using Arrays.sort(). However, sorting the arrays will result in a time complexity of O(nlogn).
